  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Showers and thunderstorms are ongoing \nacross the forecast area. A marginal (5% to 14%) risk for severe \nthunderstorm winds is in effect through tonight for severe \nthunderstorm winds for everywhere except far northern Nevada. \nAdditionally, central Nevada is in a slight (15% to 39%) chance \nof excessive rainfall while northern Nevada is in a marginal (5% \nto 14%). All combined, today is expected to be a cool and wet day,\nwith central Nevada expecting 0.25\u201d to 0.5\u201d through midnight. \nLocally higher accumulations are possible with thunderstorms, as \nwell as localized flash flooding. Storms die down overnight before\nreturning tomorrow, although much weaker and more spread out. \nExcessive rainfall remains a marginal threat, along with localized\nflash flooding. The upper level trough that has been driving \nthese storms lifts out of Nevada for the weekend with southwest \nflow taking its place. This is expected to mostly cut off the \nmoisture flow and result in a dry weekend. Models still can\u2019t \nagree on the forecast for next week, with the sticking point being\nthe path of an upper level low pressure center. The further south\nsolution would see it clip northern Nevada and would be a much \ncooler and potentially wetter solution than the one keeping it \nnear the Canadian border, however it is not clear which solution \nis more likely at this time.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in showers and\nthunderstorms through tomorrow. Moderate confidence in a dry\nweekend. Low confidence in the forecast to start next week. No\nchanges from the NBM.",
